  <subsection xml:id="introduction_arrays">
    <title>Arrays</title>

    <p>An <term>array</term> is an ordered collection of zero or more objects. The following example shows the <c>arrayOf</c> function, which is used for creating arrays if you want to specify each initial value.</p>

    <program language="kotlin">
      <code>
<xi:include parse="text" href="samplecode/src/main/kotlin/ArrayOf.kt"/>
    </code>
    </program>

    <p>Arrays can also be created with a constructor to the <c>Array</c> class, which is convenient when you don't want to specify each initial value individually. Here are examples that create arrays of a specified size, and initialize all of the values to a particular value.</p>

    <program language="kotlin">
      <code>
<xi:include parse="text" href="samplecode/src/main/kotlin/ArrayNew.kt"/>
    </code>
    </program>

    <p>Arrays have a fixed number of elements; they cannot shrink or expand without doing a lot of extra work. To get around this problem, we need to investigate Kotlin collections.</p>
  </subsection>
</section>
